{% extends "base.html.twig" %}

{% block title %}Getting Started{% endblock %}
{% block description %}Get started with Waaseyaa: create a project, migrate, start the server, and create your first content.{% endblock %}

{% block content %}
<section class="page-header">
    <h1>Getting Started</h1>
    <p>From zero to a running Waaseyaa application with content you can query.</p>
</section>

<section class="content-section">
    <h2>Prerequisites</h2>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>PHP 8.4+</strong> with extensions: <code>pdo_sqlite</code>, <code>mbstring</code>, <code>json</code>, <code>openssl</code></li>
        <li><strong>Composer 2.x</strong> (<a href="https://getcomposer.org">getcomposer.org</a>)</li>
        <li><strong>SQLite 3</strong> (the default database)</li>
    </ul>
</section>

<section class="content-section">
    <h2>Create a New Project</h2>
    <pre><code class="language-bash">composer create-project waaseyaa/waaseyaa my-app --stability=alpha
cd my-app</code></pre>
    <p>The <code>--stability=alpha</code> flag is required during the pre-release phase. It goes away at v1.0.</p>
    <p>That scaffolds a full Waaseyaa application from the skeleton. Your project now has config files, a CLI binary, templates, and directory stubs for entities, controllers, and service providers.</p>
</section>

<section class="content-section">
    <h2>Run Migrations</h2>
    <pre><code class="language-bash">php bin/waaseyaa migrate</code></pre>
    <p>This creates a SQLite database at <code>waaseyaa.sqlite</code> in your project root and runs all pending migrations. The skeleton ships with entity types for nodes, taxonomy terms, media, menus, and users. All of their tables are created here.</p>
</section>

<section class="content-section">
    <h2>Start the Dev Server</h2>
    <pre><code class="language-bash">php bin/waaseyaa serve</code></pre>
    <p>Open <code>http://127.0.0.1:8080</code> in your browser. You should see the Waaseyaa welcome page with links to the admin, API, and documentation.</p>
</section>

<section class="content-section">
    <h2>Explore Entity Types</h2>
    <p>Open a second terminal. The skeleton ships with 11 entity types out of the box:</p>
    <pre><code class="language-bash">php bin/waaseyaa entity-type:list</code></pre>
    <p>You'll see <code>node</code>, <code>taxonomy_term</code>, <code>media</code>, <code>menu</code>, <code>user</code>, and others. These are the content building blocks that come with the framework packages.</p>
</section>

<section class="content-section">
    <h2>Create Your First Content</h2>
    <pre><code class="language-bash">php bin/waaseyaa entity:create node</code></pre>
    <p>The CLI walks you through setting field values. Once created, verify it exists:</p>
    <pre><code class="language-bash">php bin/waaseyaa entity:list node</code></pre>
    <p>Your node is stored in SQLite. You can also view it through the JSON:API:</p>
    <pre><code class="language-bash">curl -s http://localhost:8080/api/node | jq</code></pre>
    <p>That returns a spec-compliant JSON:API response. Filtering, sorting, pagination, and relationship includes all work out of the box. Note that the default access control is deny-unless-granted, so anonymous requests will only see entities that have been explicitly made public.</p>
</section>

<section class="content-section">
    <h2>Project Structure</h2>
    <pre><code>my-app/
├── bin/
│   └── waaseyaa              # CLI binary
├── config/
│   ├── waaseyaa.php          # Framework configuration
│   ├── entity-types.php      # Custom entity type registration
│   └── services.php          # Service overrides
├── public/
│   └── index.php             # Front controller
├── src/
│   ├── Access/               # Authorization policies
│   ├── Controller/           # HTTP controllers
│   ├── Entity/               # Your entity classes
│   ├── Provider/             # Service providers (DI, routing)
│   ├── Domain/               # Domain logic by bounded context
│   └── Seed/                 # Seeders for dev/local bootstrap
├── templates/                # Twig templates
├── storage/                  # Runtime storage
└── waaseyaa.sqlite           # SQLite database (after migrate)</code></pre>
    <p>Configuration lives in <code>config/</code>. Your application code goes under <code>src/</code>. The front controller at <code>public/index.php</code> boots the HTTP kernel. The CLI binary at <code>bin/waaseyaa</code> boots the console kernel.</p>
</section>

<section class="content-section">
    <h2>What's Next</h2>
    <p>You have a running application with built-in entity types and a JSON:API. From here:</p>
